overall,  I found the correct continuation but missed that I had to force a perpetual check at the end, I also had a bit of visualisation fog, forgetting that the bishop had already been taken and hanging my queen in one line. I need to do more blindfold games. At first I struggled finding the right candidate move, but when I systematically looked for checks captures and threats first the right first candidate move appeared and it looked very promising, so I went deeper on that candidate move and it was correct. Going forward, I should always first expand candidate moves with CCT, rather than just looking for things blindly out of thin air, once I systematically isolated forcing moves, I found the right candidate. } 1. Nf6+! {I got this line all correct up until the end where I had to realise the best thing white can do is force a perpetual check rather than play on down a rook. } gxf6  (1... Qxf6  2. Bxc6+ Bd7  (2... Kd8  3. Bxb7 {this is what I saw after kd8, and I correctly evaluated that white should be better as the position opens up and the king is stranded in the centre, but I missed a winning intermediate move Qc5! infiltrating on the dark squares. If black takes the bishop then white can win the rook after Qb6 check and forcing the king back.  }  (3. Qc5!!  bxc6 4. Qb6+ Kd7  (4... Kc8  5. Qxc6+ Kd8 6. Qxa8+) 5. Qb7+ Kd8 6. Qxa8+) Rb8 4. Bxa6) 3. Bxd7+  (3. Bxb7  Rb8 4. Qc7 Qd8 {I saw this and misevaluated this as about equal as I thought black defends fine, but it's actually a mate in 3 after Qd8, factoring in black's king is just stuck in the centre and all my pieces are swarrming it. } 5. Qe5+ Be6 6. Bc6+) Kxd7 {I evaluated this as better for black as black can untangle and still is up an exchange, I was correct in this line!

&nbsp;}) 2. Bxc6+ bxc6 3. Qxc6+ Bd7 4. Qe4+  (4. Qxa8?! {this is what I saw in my head during calculations, completely forgetting that my bishop had already been taken and isn't on g2 anymore! This obviously just hangs an undefended queen. Visualisation fog. }  ) Be6 5. Qc6+ {This is the solution, white has to force a perpetual check as white is down too much material. } *

